A||||12||fiero56||Worst tires in snow ever||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-18-2000||11:16 PM||||The '86 SE I recently purchased has brand new, very low mileage, tires mounted on the tech wheels. Daytona Radial S/R I think. These things are terrible in snow, slush, or ice. Under braking the wheels just plow through the snow locked up, they don't corner at all, and it is impossible to get the car moving with these tires on. My Formula with the Pirelli HPs on it could run circles around my SE in the snow. Tonight we got another couple inches of snow, so I bought 80lbs worth of salt pellets and threw the bags in the front compartment. The added weight does help some, it got rid of that terrible understeer(no oversteer either), and I can corner while braking, somewhat. <BR>When these tires are up for replacement I will stick a set of HPs on there. They are cheap, are rated at 280TW(I think) so they are pretty sticky for an a/s tire. Anybody know of any other tires to stay away from for winter driving.||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||12-19-2000||02:31 AM||||yup, don't try racing slicks!! (just kiddin with ya Drew!)||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||12-19-2000||02:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>Anybody know of any other tires to stay away from for winter driving.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>firestone. hehe j/k  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>no flames <P><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||12-19-2000||05:07 AM||||Hmmmm ... I've got the same tires on my coupe, Drew, and I've been worried about having to drive mine in the snow.  I drove it this morning with little to no problems.<P>Maybe you shouldn't do your Mario Andretti impersonation down 741 when it hasn't been cleared!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   j/k||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||12-19-2000||05:32 AM||||I would not use salt pellets as ballast.  If the bags rip open and the salt gets out, you will corrode the front compartment very quickly.  You can get sand ballast for that very thing.<P>I don't drive in the winter with the Fiero.  The 215/60 tires had limited grip and the car rides low, the front is a snow plow.  You have to be real light on the throttle.  The front 205's act like skis.  Wide tires ride on top of the snow.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||12-19-2000||10:08 AM||||Problem with Pirelli HP is... can you still get them?  I have them on my 323 GTX and used to have them on my Fiero.  I agree, good tire for the cost, although they seem to wear fast.  But when the HP's on m Fiero were ready for replacement, I could not find them. Best of my knowledge, they were only available from Sears, right? Sears says discontinued.<BR>I replaced mine with Bridgestone Turanza H Revo's. Kinda spendy (+- $500 for the set) but they have been behaving very well in the ice storm we have been dealing with for the past week. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||12-19-2000||01:23 PM||||HPs do wear fast. My rears have about 15,000 miles and about 50% wear. But they are only $50 a piece around here. I just bought a new pair for the front a few months ago(August??). I buy them from the local NTB(Sears without the name) without any problem. Maybe you looked for a different size. They are only available in a few sizes. I buy 205/60/15 and 215/60/15. HPs were on my Formula when I bought it, and liked them so I bought new HPs when the "originals" wore out.||||reg||1
Z||000007||jmw||12-19-2000||02:59 PM||||The worst? Eagle GTs. I used to live in Michigan and had to drive around with boat anchors in the trunk just to keep the tires on the road. Not just in snow - rain too. And not just heavy rain - any rain. They hydroplaned like crazy. Never tried putting anchors in the front -- always seemed to be the back sliding around...||||reg||13
Z||000008||wahoo4u||12-19-2000||03:35 PM||||I so agree with you on the tires.  I am running the same tire on an 86 SE  Those tires don't do worth a darn in the winter.  They handle great in the summer though.  Try running them at a little lower pressure so that you have more surface on the road.  Just not to low so you don't damage the sidewalls.  It my help you some.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||12-19-2000||07:01 PM||||Drew--Pirellis are a good choice.  If you remember, I have Pirelli P4000 touring tires on the Grape Ape.  We've been buried in snow up here for the better part of a week and I haven't had any trouble (except when I wanted trouble--heh heh).  I'm running 205/60/15s.  Somewhere around a hundred bucks a piece up here.  P Zeros are actually supposed to be outstanding in the snow (surprising for a completely high performance tire) but at $250+ each, I think I'll just go slower.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Gonzo_442||12-20-2000||12:47 AM||||anyone had any experience with the pirelli p6000?<P>i was looking at them when the need arises to install new tires.<P>going to be mounted on a set of 16" rims, not stocks.<BR>--<BR>g||||reg||1
Z||000011||stimpy||12-20-2000||01:01 AM||||I want to say that that is the tire my dad has on his GT.  He really likes them. The last time I drove it, it seemed to hook up very well for a touring tire.  I still like my Bridestones though.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Uaana||12-20-2000||03:48 AM||||Has anyone tried using any of the following.<BR>(I just got my new rims and am still deciding<BR>Yokohama Avid's<BR>Dunlop D60A2's<BR>B/FG's TA's<BR>And am also deciding between 205's and 215's<BR>Mind you I drive year round in weather that goes from 115 in summer to -40 in winter.||||reg||1
